H. P. Lovecraft’s lasting influence on fiction is enormous, even though many readers still react to his work with the same frustration you mentioned. His main legacy is not that he made horror “scarier” in a simple sense, but that he changed the scale and texture of fear in fiction.

He helped popularize a kind of horror where:

- **the universe is indifferent**, not morally ordered;
- **human beings are small and fragile** rather than central and special;
- **knowledge itself can be dangerous**;
- and **the most frightening things are often beyond clear description**.

That approach influenced a huge range of later writers and genres. You can see his impact in:

- **modern horror**, especially stories that emphasize cosmic dread, forbidden knowledge, and psychological breakdown;
- **fantasy and science fiction**, where ancient civilizations, alien gods, and lost grim lore became common tropes;
- **video games, films, comics, and tabletop RPGs**, many of which borrow his imagery and themes;
- and even **mainstream thriller and literary fiction**, which sometimes adopt his sense of creeping dread and unseen menace.

At the same time, his influence is complicated. Lovecraft was a brilliant stylist in some respects, but he also wrote with deep personal prejudices that shaped his work in ugly ways. So his legacy is twofold: he was a major architect of modern weird fiction, but one whose ideas and language many later creators have had to reject, revise, or transform.

If you prefer domestic realist fiction, it may help to think of Lovecraft less as someone who wrote about everyday human life and more as someone who pushed fiction toward **the collapse of everyday life under vast, unknowable pressure**. That’s why he remains influential: not because everyone loves his stories, but because he gave later writers a powerful blueprint for turning fear into atmosphere, scale, and existential unease.
